 "result": "I've written the Dockerfile and the two files it needs. I couldn't build or test anything in this session, so the image has never been built and the entrypoint script's tests have never been run.\n\nFiles created:\n\n- **`Dockerfile`** builds the app in three steps:\n  - **Web app:** built with Node 24. Its build also copies in `docs/`, because the in-app guide is generated from those pages.\n  - **Program:** a Go 1.26 build, cross-compiled for the target platform. The version comes from `VERSION`, and you can pass optional build args (`VERSION`, `TMDB_API_KEY`, `OPENSUBTITLES_API_KEY`, `TRAKT_CLIENT_ID`, `SIMKL_CLIENT_ID`) to build keys in. I named this step `build` and put the program at `/out/app` because `tools/push-update.sh` expects both.\n  - **Final image:** Alpine 3.21 with the tools the app calls (`par2`, `7z`, plus `tini`, `su-exec`, certificates and time zones). The program is at `/app/app`, settings live in `/config`, it listens on port 8264 (torrents on 58264 TCP/UDP), and a health check calls `/api/version`.\n- **`docker/entrypoint.sh`** is the startup script. The code already expects it at that path: the self-update tests in `internal/selfupdate/entrypoint_test.go` run it. It:\n  - sets up folder ownership for `PUID`/`PGID`, then runs the app as that user;\n  - applies `UMASK`;\n  - tells the app which version is in the image;\n  - decides whether to start an update installed from inside the app instead of the image's own program. I wrote this part to match what those tests check: the checksum, version and platform checks, the \"stopped 3 times in a row\" fallback, and the skip switch.\n- **`.dockerignore`** keeps `.git`, `.env`, local data folders and build output out of the build.\n\nThings to check when you first build:\n\n- **`7z`:** I couldn't confirm that Alpine 3.21 still has the `p7zip` package, so the Dockerfile falls back to the `7zip` package and links `7zz` to `7z` if needed.\n- **Existing media folders:** only the config folder is given to `PUID`/`PGID`. Media folders are only created, with that owner, when they don't already exist; folders you already have are left alone.\n\nTo build and run, use `docker build -t mediarium .`, then the example `docker run` command at the top of the Dockerfile. Running `go test ./internal/selfupdate/` will test the startup script.",
